WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:05.629
Thor: Ragnarok had a pretty epic ending with
a nice set-up for Thor and the Revengers going

00:00:05.629 --> 00:00:07.240
into Avengers: Infinity War.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:11.269
But recently the filmmakers revealed some
interesting alternate endings, some of which

00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:14.870
were filmed and others just considered, for
the third Thor movie.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:18.410
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and today I'm talking endings we never got

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:22.930
to see, crucial plot changes, plus another
stack of deleted scenes, rejected concepts,

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:27.439
and end credit scenes that I've found out
about since my first video on Thor: Ragnarok's

00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:28.609
deleted scenes.

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:32.460
Don't forget I've still got some cool Marvel
merch to give away, so make sure you're a

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.020
subscriber and comment for your chance to
win.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:40.020
And obviously, I'm discussing spoilers, so
take care if you haven't seen the movie yet.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.440
In an alternate post-credits scene to the
one we saw in the film, the Grandmaster and

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.440
his right-hand-woman Topaz were going to stow
away on the ship with the Asgardians at the

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:49.650
end of the movie.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:54.300
To conceal their identity, the dictatorial
duo would have put blankets over their heads,

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.989
which really reminds me of the scene where
Thor does the same during the Hulk Parade,

00:00:57.989 --> 00:01:01.800
and also of the moment that Skurge disguised
himself with a hooded cape and tried to stow

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.580
away on the ship leaving Asgard, that is,
until he had an attack of conscience.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:10.140
This version of the end-credits would have
left the Grandmaster in a very different situation

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:11.720
to the final film.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:15.750
Instead of still being on Sakaar facing his
rebellious subjects, this alternate scene

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:19.940
would have meant that, just like the Asgardians
he's travelling with, the Grandmaster's journey

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:24.780
to Earth would have been interrupted by the
arrival of Thanos's craft, the Sanctuary 2.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.240
It would have been interesting to see these
two characters cross paths, as their comic-book

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.010
counterparts have done, for example, when
the Grandmaster lost his Mind Gem to Thanos.

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:37.530
By the way, the Grandmaster's alternate post-credits
scene was actually filmed, so hopefully we'll

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.360
get to see it on the DVD release.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:45.180
Although the final movie sees Asgard destroyed,
in an interview with Empire, Taika Waititi

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.790
revealed that the last act of the movie could
have been completely different to what we

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:50.050
saw on screen.

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:54.420
Indeed, during early script development, Asgard
wasn't going to be torn apart and burned to

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:58.590
bits; instead, it was actually going to be
saved from destruction, something I think

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:03.071
many comic book fans wouldn't have been especially
happy to see on screen, given that Ragnarok

00:02:03.071 --> 00:02:05.660
is supposed to be a cycle of death and rebirth.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:09.810
In the same interview, Waititi also revealed
that Valhalla was originally going to be a

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:13.750
significant part of the film, 'with a part
of the journey actually [going] to Valhalla,

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.390
[and …] a big showdown there.'

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:18.920
And I wonder if that version of the script
would have involved Hela trying to extend

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:23.060
her domain from Hel to Valhalla, as she does
in the comics.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:27.560
Hela's reveal as Odin's first-born child was
one of the few secrets that was pretty well-kept

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.440
until close to the movie's release.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.630
However, in early versions of the script,
the reveal of Hela as Thor's sister actually

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.400
happened later in the story.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.480
And in the original draft, it was Valkyrie
who told Thor that Hela was his sister when

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.130
they were together in Hulk's suite on Sakaar.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:46.490
In the final script though, Odin's dark secret
that Hela was the true heir to the throne

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:50.561
of Asgard was moved earlier in order to avoid
a possible comparison with Star Wars: The

00:02:50.561 --> 00:02:55.040
Empire Strikes Back and the reveal of Luke
Skywalker's father at the end of that movie.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:59.430
And the person revealing that information
was changed to Odin as they felt it made better

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.380
use of Anthony Hopkins' limited screentime.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:07.069
Director Taika Waititi also felt that Hela
being a secret family member wasn't quite

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:11.470
as important as the dark truth it revealed
about how Odin had constructed a false history

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:13.260
about himself and Asgard.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:17.380
Which is another reason Waititi wanted to
get the sister reveal out of the way earlier

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:18.380
in the movie.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:23.350
Hela's look and costume also underwent several
revisions and there were some fascinating

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:24.600
early versions.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.800
As well this much darker version of Hela and
her costume, her headdress was also going

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.260
to be weaponised.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:34.540
In an interview with Vanity Fair, VFX supervisor
Jake Morrison mentioned that an early design

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:39.130
of Hela's headgear included antlers that could
"turn into face-mask shields […] almost

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:41.380
like samurai-style outfits".

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:44.910
I'm guessing these might have been scrapped
because when producers have got expensive,

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:49.220
famous actors for a big movie, they don't
necessarily want them to spend a lot of screentime

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:50.810
with their faces covered up.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:55.910
While we didn't get those extra features on
Hela's costume, we still saw her in some pretty

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:58.709
fantastic action scenes that show off her
incredible power.

00:03:58.709 --> 00:04:02.849
For example, the amazing scene where Hela
takes out the entire elite Asgardian army

00:04:02.849 --> 00:04:07.020
single-handedly is an extraordinary demonstration
of her capabilities.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.690
But there were actually multiple versions
of this scene.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:14.099
Originally, it was meant to be even longer
but was cut back to avoid repetition and extending

00:04:14.099 --> 00:04:15.440
production days.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.530
On top of that, Jake Morrison explained that
it was actually filmed as a single continuous

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:23.630
action shot in which "a revolving camera"
tracked Hela "as she [ploughed] through and

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:24.770
killed everybody."

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:29.070
I think a single-camera unbroken take of that
scene could have been amazing to watch.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:33.070
However, I can imagine it would have been
really difficult to get just right and even

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:36.850
tiny errors in the stunt choreography or timing
could have spoiled it.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:40.961
Morrison has said that the scene was later
re-edited to make it more "percussive", which

00:04:40.961 --> 00:04:44.860
sounds like it perhaps wasn't quite up-to-scratch
to work as a single shot.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:50.790
And we almost got a scene between Hela and
the Destroyer, the Asgardian automaton from

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:51.790
the first Thor movie.

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:55.320
In the original draft, when Hela is looking
for Heimdall's sword so she can operate the

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.600
Bifrost Bridge, she goes to look inside the
weapons vault.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:03.130
As she enters the room, the Destroyer is activated
and tries to stop her, but Hela just rips

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:04.130
it apart.

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:08.190
The scene was cut as it was mostly intended
to be a call-back to the first movie and,

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:10.220
in the end, felt unnecessary.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:14.280
By the way, if you're a Hela fan who's hoping
she somehow survived at the end of the film,

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:18.380
check out my new Avengers: Infinity War theory
video about why I think there's a big chance

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:20.410
we'll see Hela back in that movie.

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:25.550
Speaking to Yahoo, screenwriter Eric Pearson
revealed that when he came on to the project,

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:29.440
the original writers had already included
a romantic relationship between Valkyrie and

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:30.600
Thor in the script.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:34.780
Personally, I'm happy that Pearson decided
to scrap that part of the plot, and instead

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:39.620
ensured that Valkyrie had 'her own story',
rather than just some romantic subplot.

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:43.390
And I think it's much more interesting that
Valkyrie's story 'became more about the mutual

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:47.550
respect [between her and Thor], and also dealing
with her PTSD.'

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:51.820
Now, I already explained a lot of deleted
scenes in my previous video, but since then,

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:55.949
there's some new details come out about other
deleted scenes which I want to discuss.

00:05:55.949 --> 00:05:59.370
One especially great deleted scene that I
know many fans would have loved to see was

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:01.000
a call-back to The Avengers.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.610
In a scene after the Revengers land on the
Rainbow Bridge, there would have been a moment

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:08.800
where they stand there for a second and then
Hulk punches Loki right out of the frame,

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:12.449
just like he did to Thor during the Battle
of New York in The Avengers.

00:06:12.449 --> 00:06:16.040
This would have been pretty funny to see in
the movie as Loki suspected he was in trouble

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:19.080
when he first saw Hulk in the Grandmaster's
arena.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:22.780
And this deleted scene would have been even
funnier because during the arena scene, Loki

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:28.199
himself cried out 'Yes, that's how it feels',
when Hulk smashed Thor around, like he'd done

00:06:28.199 --> 00:06:29.760
to Loki in The Avengers.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.560
Sadly, Waititi has said the scene with Loki
getting punched out of frame was cut for time,

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.360
though it might appear on the DVD.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:40.000
But I wonder whether it was part of other
missing scenes on the Rainbow Bridge that

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.500
we never got to see, like the Revengers lining
up to face off against Hela, plus various

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:45.850
Hela fight moves.

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:50.580
There was another funny scene in the trailers
of Banner and Thor talking about muscles and

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.389
brains that sadly didn't make it in.

00:06:52.389 --> 00:06:54.889
"Biggest muscle in the body."

00:06:54.889 --> 00:06:57.290
"I've got lots of muscles so technically more
brains!"

00:06:57.290 --> 00:07:01.889
This moment would have been especially sweet
since it was a call-back to Thor's mockumentary

00:07:01.889 --> 00:07:04.210
video with Darryl where he makes a similar
joke.

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:05.230
"Ooh, this is knowledge.

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:06.290
Knowledge is power.

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:07.290
No it's not.

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:08.370
Knowledge is not power.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:09.370
That's power.

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:11.100
The brain is a muscle.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:12.100
And that's a muscle.

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:13.100
And that's a muscle.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:14.370
And they are all muscles.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:19.229
This is all muscle, so I am covered in brains,
and I've more brains than you."

00:07:19.229 --> 00:07:23.389
Banner and Thor were also together in yet
another humorous but slightly gross "noodle

00:07:23.389 --> 00:07:25.229
worm" scene that ended up cut.

00:07:25.229 --> 00:07:29.260
According to screenwriter Eric Pearson's interview
with Yahoo, there "was an emotional check-in

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:33.460
moment with Bruce Banner and Thor before the
finale, with Banner eating alien food and

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:34.509
trying to be serious.

00:07:34.509 --> 00:07:39.669
It [looks like] spaghetti, but then he realizes
the thing he's eating is alive [on the end]."

00:07:39.669 --> 00:07:43.650
Despite multiple rewrites of what Pearson
called a "pivotal character moment", the scene

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:46.590
ended up being canned as it "crushed the momentum"
of the movie.

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:51.710
Now, there was already a deleted snippet from
Valkyrie's flashback scene that we saw teased

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:55.030
in the trailers of Hela turning nonchalantly
to the camera.

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:58.221
But there was another moment missing from
the flashback that looks like it could have

00:07:58.221 --> 00:08:02.290
explained exactly how Valkyrie survived the
massacre of the Valkyrior at the hands of

00:08:02.290 --> 00:08:03.290
Hela.

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:07.530
VFX studio Rising Sun Pictures, who worked
on the flashback scene, revealed that the

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:11.750
reason Valkyrie survived is that she fell
into a portal and escaped.

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:15.700
You do see Valkyrie falling back at the very
end of the flashback but there's no obvious

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:19.750
portal behind her, which makes me think the
scene was cut a little short.

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:23.070
And I wonder whether it was going through
that portal that meant she ended up on Sakaar.

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:27.820
Something which I've been asked about is why
Thor has both eyes in the trailer when in

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:29.570
the movie he loses one to Hela.

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:34.029
Plus, if you've seen the Infinity War footage
from D23 and Comic Con, you'll know that Thor

00:08:34.029 --> 00:08:36.379
had both his eyes intact there too.

00:08:36.379 --> 00:08:41.309
The simple answer is that Marvel wanted to
avoid spoiling the fact Thor loses his eye,

00:08:41.309 --> 00:08:45.129
a plot point that comes near the end of the
film, so they made sure he appeared with both

00:08:45.129 --> 00:08:48.920
eyes in the marketing materials for Ragnarok
and Infinity War.

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:53.139
Thor losing his eye was always in the original
story and Waititi has talked about it being

00:08:53.139 --> 00:08:57.899
part of stripping down Thor's character, starting
with the loss of his father, then his hammer,

00:08:57.899 --> 00:09:00.550
his eye, and finally his homeland Asgard.

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:04.879
The eye patch that Thor puts on at the end
is a symbolic reference to his father Odin

00:09:04.879 --> 00:09:08.720
as well as a nod to the comics; and Chris
Hemsworth has confirmed that this is the look

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:11.070
we'll see for Thor in Avengers: Infinity War.

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:14.939
So, did you like the way that Thor: Ragnarok
ended?

00:09:14.939 --> 00:09:18.560
And would you like to have seen any of the
deleted scenes included in the movie?

